# Line endings
#
# The repo had no .gitattributes at all, so every checkout got whatever the
# machine's core.autocrlf happened to say. On the primary Windows machine the
# system setting is autocrlf=true, which writes CRLF into the working tree
# while the blobs stay LF — so any tool that rewrites a file byte-for-byte
# (a script, a formatter, an agent edit) can silently flip a whole file's
# endings and produce a diff that touches every line.
#
# Normalise to LF in the index, everywhere. Nothing here builds with a tool
# that requires CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only shells stay CRLF: cmd.exe mis-parses an LF .bat/.cmd.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Sources and project files (explicit, so a new extension is never guessed).
*.cs        text diff=csharp
*.csproj    text
*.sln       text eol=crlf
*.props     text
*.targets   text
*.json      text
*.md        text
*.yml       text
*.yaml      text
*.py        text
*.txt       text
*.h         text diff=cpp
*.hpp       text diff=cpp
*.c         text diff=cpp
*.cpp       text diff=cpp
*.cmake     text
CMakeLists.txt text

# Binary: never diffed, never munged.
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.dll   binary
*.exe   binary
*.lib   binary
*.pdb   binary
*.so    binary
*.dylib binary
*.wav   binary
*.flac  binary
*.mp3   binary
*.ogg   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.ico   binary
*.onnx  binary
*.ckpt  binary
*.nupkg binary
*.snupkg binary

# NOTE: the tree is NOT renormalised in the change that added this file — a
# whole-repo `git add --renormalize .` collides with every branch in flight.
# Run it as its own follow-up commit, on a quiet tree:
#
#     git add --renormalize .
#     git commit -m "chore: renormalise line endings to LF"
